  *
- * Parses PAMF container headers to extract stream information.
- * The actual PAMF header format is parsed to provide correct stream
- * counts and codec parameters to cellDmux/cellVdec/cellAdec.
+ * Reads a PAMF header in guest memory. Layout (big-endian):
+ *
+ *   0x00 "PAMF"   0x04 version ("0041")
+ *   0x08 u32 header size in 2048-byte sectors (= start of the program stream)
+ *   0x0C u32 program-stream size in sectors
+ *   0x50 u32 table size
+ *   0x56 u16 + 0x58 u32  first PTS (33 bits, 90 kHz)
+ *   0x5C u16 + 0x5E u32  last PTS
+ *   0x62 u32 mux rate bound (units of 400 bit/s)
+ *   0x6D u8  number of streams
+ *   0x88 stream table, 0x30 bytes per stream:
+ *        +0 coding type (0x1B AVC, 0x02 M2V, 0xDC ATRAC3plus, 0x80 LPCM,
+ *           0x81 AC3, 0xDD user data)
+ *        +4 stream id (0xE0 video, 0xBD private 1), +5 private sub-stream id
+ *        +8 u32 offset of this stream's entry-point table in the header
+ *        +0xC u32 number of entry points
+ *        +0x10 32 bytes of codec information
+ *   Entry point, 12 bytes: u16 (indexN in the top 2 bits, then the offset of
+ *   the n-th reference picture in sectors, minus one, in the low 13), u16 + u32
+ *   PTS, u32 offset from the start of the stream in sectors.
+ *
+ * The title's movie files are encrypted on disk; it decrypts the header
+ * itself and hands it here. PAMF_DUMP=<dir> writes each header it is given
+ * there as pamf_<n>.bin (game data: keep it in scratch/).
  */
 
